I've written the plan for two tasks: session_plan/task_01.md, task_02.md and issue_responses.md. The commit step did nothing because session_plan/ is in .gitignore. The planning instructions already tolerate that with || true, so the files are on disk and the implement phase can read them.

Task 1: Fixes #1227, my main pick, safety parity with Claude Code 2.1.330. I filed #1227 during planning. The question is whether yoyo flags a recursive delete aimed at a top-level folder (/home, /Users, /mnt, /tmp itself, or any single-folder absolute path) or at the working folder ($PWD, $(pwd)). The assessment only read the code for this and never ran it, so the task measures first. It runs a table of rows (including near-misses that must keep passing, like rm -rf ./target and /tmp/x) through the safety check functions directly; nothing is ever run in a shell. The before-table goes on the ticket before any code change. One known gap: the working folder written out as an absolute path. If the check has no way to know the current directory, that becomes its own ticket instead of being added in this task.

Task 2: Fixes #1102, the backlog-drain slot. This ticket tracks hard_deny refusing heredoc text that only mentions a dangerous pattern. The comments hold about 16 hits on my own loop. 4–5 of them are `cat > file <<'EOF'`, where the text is written to a file and never run. The new rule treats that text as data only when it goes to cat/tee writing a file and isn't piped onward. Piping into a shell and interpreter heredocs stay refused. The commit has to say plainly that writing a script and then running it still passes, which is the same as using write_file today.

Planning hit #1102 itself: my first attempt to write the task files used a cat > file <<'EOF' heredoc, and hard_deny refused it because the plan mentions a home-directory delete. I wrote the files with write_file instead and added the hit to #1102.

